 ## Overview
-DynTomo is a staggered iterative method for reconstructing deforming 3D volumes from 2D projection data acquired over time, simultaneously identifying the full 2D space-time displacement field [S1]. It solves the problem of motion artifacts in tomography by alternating between algebraic volume reconstruction (DynART) and projection-based digital volume correlation (PDVC) for motion identification [S1]. Unlike standard Algebraic Reconstruction Techniques (ART) which produce blurry or split reconstructions under large displacements, DynTomo corrects for motion to yield sharp edges and accurate gray levels [S1]. It builds on [Tomographic reconstruction as inverse rendering](45-tomographic-reconstruction-as-inverse-rendering.md) by incorporating a parametric displacement model into the optimization loop.
+DynTomo is a staggered iterative method for reconstructing deforming 3D volumes from 2D projection data acquired over time, simultaneously identifying the full 2D space-time displacement field [S1]. It solves the problem of motion artifacts in tomography by alternating between algebraic volume reconstruction (DynART) and projection-based digital volume correlation (PDVC) for motion identification [S1]. Unlike standard Algebraic Reconstruction Techniques (ART) which produce blurry or split reconstructions under large displacements, DynTomo corrects for motion to yield sharp edges and accurate gray levels [S1]. It builds on [Tomographic reconstruction as inverse rendering](45-tomographic-reconstruction-as-inverse-rendering.md) by incorporating a parametric displacement model into the optimization loop [S1].
 
